// M13pS1 — the one-column stream-emitter prototype (LS1/LS2, the v4 device-RUN raster). Walks a
// column's run-lists (ceiling band list, ONE wall run, floor band list) and emits each run as
// `byte.emit(count)` + `cm.emit(colour-index)` through the REAL device-emit tables (present.fj's
// `begin_frame_stream` + doomfj.lut_generator.generate_emit_dispatch_table_fj tables named "byte"/
// "cm" by the includer — see M13pS0). This is the body pS2 wires per-column, ×160, after pass-1;
// pS1 only proves + measures it against a SYNTHETIC column (fixed band data), not real pass-1/LS2
// output. @requires hex.init & stl.ptr_init (ptr_index/read_hex) — stl.startup_and_init_all covers
// both, plus the includer's "byte"/"cm" emit tables (each table's own `.init` runs via its `<label>:
// ;end … end:` header, called once at table-declaration time — see lut_generator's `_per_entry_
// emit_table`; no separate init call needed here).

ns stream {
    // Walk n RUNTIME band-list entries SEQUENTIALLY (pS2's real per-column band lists vary in length
    // — this is a genuine runtime loop, not a compile-time unroll, even though pS2's per-column CALL
    // site is unrolled ×160). `arr` holds n entries of 3 PACKED bytes each (count, cidx-low, cidx-
    // high — cidx up to 65535 covers the real E1M1 colormap's ~8,192-entry index, D11), back-to-back,
    // ONE dw-slot per byte (`;value*dw`, the SAME idiom `lut_generator.generate_byte_lut_fj`/
    // `hex.read_table_byte` use — NOT `hex.vec` register form, which is TWO dw-slots per nibble-pair
    // and needs 2x the dereferences for the same data, see the trap note below). ONE pointer walks
    // the whole array via `hex.read_byte_and_inc` (ptr_inc advances exactly one dw-slot per call,
    // packed or not — memory-model note: a bit/hex/byte all fit in one dw-slot's data bits, so a
    // packed BYTE read gets 8 data bits per dw-slot vs a nibble read's 4 — half the dereferences for
    // the same payload). Per-entry cost is ONLY 3 dereferences (1 byte for count + 2 bytes for cidx)
    // + the two emits, no per-entry index recompute.
    // ⚠ TRAP #1 (cost ~20min): `hex.read_hex n, dst, ptr` (the STL's own n-nibble multi-read) does
    // NOT net-advance ptr — its body is `rep(n,i) read_hex_and_inc ...` followed by a compensating
    // `ptr_sub ptr, n`, i.e. it PEEKS n nibbles at the CURRENT position and restores ptr (by design,
    // so repeated reads of the same field don't drift). Using it here made every entry re-read the
    // array's FIRST nibbles forever (byte-exact test caught it immediately — a constant-colour column
    // instead of the synthetic bands).
    // ⚠ TRAP #2 (cost ~15min): switching to `read_byte_and_inc` while the DATA was still declared as
    // `hex.vec` register form (nibble-per-dw-slot) reads garbage — packed-byte reads expect ONE
    // dw-slot per BYTE (8 data bits), not per nibble (4 data bits); mixing the two encodings is
    // exactly the register-vs-packed trap the flipjump-dev skill warns about. Fix: declare the array
    // with `;value*dw` packed-byte ops (see the test's `_band_list_fj`/measure script's `_band_list`).
    // (v0 of this macro used `hex.ptr_index` per field per entry — MEASURED at ~7.8k ops/run, ~10x
    // the ~600-900 LS1 estimate, because ptr_index is O(w) per call; v1 (nibble-wise read_hex_and_inc,
    // register-form data) measured ~3.9k ops/run; this packed-byte version measures ~2.1k ops/run —
    // see scratchpad/measure_stream_column.py for the full ladder.) Per entry: byte.emit(count) then
    // cm.emit(colour) — mirrors the pS2 spec's "per band ... byte.emit(...) then cm.emit(...)"
    // (docs/m13p-procedural-plan.md, the pS section).
    def emit_band_list arr, n \
            @ i, ptr, cnt, cidx, loop, body, done, skip {
        hex.set w/4, ptr, arr
        hex.zero w/4, i
      loop:
        hex.cmp 2, i, n, body, done, done
      body:
        hex.read_byte_and_inc cnt, ptr                    // cnt = *ptr (packed byte); ptr += dw
        byte.emit cnt
        rep(2, k) hex.read_byte_and_inc cidx + 2*k*dw, ptr // cidx = *ptr (2 packed bytes); ptr += 2*dw
        cm.emit cidx
        hex.inc w/4, i
        ;loop
      done:
        ;skip                                     // skip past the trailing @-local data (lesson #2)
      i: hex.vec w/4
      ptr: hex.vec w/4
      cnt: hex.vec 2
      cidx: hex.vec 4
      skip:
    }

    // One column's 3 windows: ceiling band list [0,cexcl) · ONE wall run [cexcl,fstart) · floor band
    // list [fstart,H). `cexcl`/`fstart` are 2-nibble RUNTIME registers (pS2 feeds these from pass-1's
    // per-column arrays, read at a compile-time column address — free). `wall_lit` is the column's W1
    // wall FINAL palette byte (col_lit, fully baked at Python emit time — light row + mode texel are
    // both per-seg constants — so the wall run needs NO runtime colormap lookup: `byte.emit`, not
    // `cm.emit`; M13pS2c). A ZERO-length wall run (an off-screen wall: cexcl==fstart) emits NOTHING —
    // mid-stream a 0-count pair is merely 2 wasted emits, but on the frame's LAST column a trailing
    // pair would arrive AFTER the device's width*height pixels completed and leak into command-byte
    // decode.
    def emit_column ceil_bands, ceil_n, cexcl, fstart, wall_lit, floor_bands, floor_n \
            @ wlen, nowall, skip {
        .emit_band_list ceil_bands, ceil_n
        hex.zero 2, wlen
        hex.mov 2, wlen, fstart
        hex.sub 2, wlen, cexcl                    // wlen = fstart - cexcl (the ONE wall run's length)
        hex.if0 2, wlen, nowall
        byte.emit wlen
        byte.emit wall_lit
      nowall:
        .emit_band_list floor_bands, floor_n
        ;skip
      wlen: hex.vec 2
      skip:
    }
}
